  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "GE-C06-sechs",
      "sequence": 219,
      "title": "sechs — \"six,\" where chs is just an x",
      "headword": "sechs",
      "romanization": "sechs",
      "gloss": "six — where chs is simply an x",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:ba44096b37f207e3",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The second hand starts here. And the sixth number quietly settles the last thing German ch can do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: sechs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sechs — \"six.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ch-none — in chs, the ch makes no throat sound at all. The pair is a plain ks, exactly the English letter x."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So sechs is zeks, and it rhymes with English sex — which is also, as it happens, the Latin word for six."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That completes the ch: raspy after a as in machen, and silent-into-ks when an s follows. Nothing else to learn about it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sechs is English six, and here the two languages have barely moved apart at all — zeks against siks. Even the Latin cousin sex is close enough to hear, because this is one of the few numbers no sound law rearranged much."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which makes it the cheapest number in the chapter: if you can say x, you can say sechs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"sechs\" — zeks, no throat rasp",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"sechs\" — *zeks*, no throat rasp]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"machen\" then \"sechs\" — the two jobs of ch",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"machen\" then \"sechs\" — the two jobs of *ch*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"sechs\" then \"six\" — barely a step apart",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"sechs\" then \"six\" — barely a step apart]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is \"six\"? (Sechs, said zeks.) What does ch do in front of an s? (Nothing — the pair is a plain ks.) And after an a? (The raspy throat sound, as in machen.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C06-sieben",
      "sequence": 220,
      "title": "sieben — \"seven,\" and the buzzing s",
      "headword": "sieben",
      "romanization": "sieben",
      "gloss": "seven — and the s that buzzes at the front of a word",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:56bcfc7e6c438d6c",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have been saying sechs as zeks without being told why. Here is the rule, on the number that needs it most."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: sieben",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sieben — \"seven.\" Two syllables: ZEE-ben."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "s-voiced — a German s in front of a vowel buzzes, like the English letter z. At the end of a word it goes back to a plain hiss."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ie-long-ee — the long ee again, as in vier."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So sieben is ZEE-ben, not SEE-ben. And it explains the two numbers before it: sechs is zeks and not seks for the same reason."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sieben is English seven, with the middle consonant softened from a v to a b — a swap those two letters make in each other's direction constantly, because they are made with the same lips."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the one number where the Latin cousin is also unmistakable: septem, which English still uses in September and in septet. Seven is the number almost nobody's language disguised."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"sieben\" — ZEE-ben, buzz the s",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"sieben\" — *ZEE-ben*, buzz the *s*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"sechs, sieben\" — both start with the buzz",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"sechs, sieben\" — both start with the buzz]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"sieben\" then \"seven\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"sieben\" then \"seven\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is \"seven\"? (Sieben, said ZEE-ben.) What does a German s do in front of a vowel? (It buzzes, like z.) And at the end of a word? (It hisses.) Which English word is sieben? (Seven — the v softened to a b.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C06-acht",
      "sequence": 221,
      "title": "acht — \"eight,\" on a rasp you already have",
      "headword": "acht",
      "romanization": "acht",
      "gloss": "eight — the rasp you already own, on a number you already know",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a57c366dfe1c903d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nothing new in the mouth for this one. You met its sound on the very first Gute Nacht."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: acht",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "acht — \"eight.\" Said akht."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ch is the raspy back-of-the-throat sound from Nacht and machen: a clean open ah, then the scrape. One syllable, no vowel after it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "acht is English eight — and the pairing is easier to believe once you notice that English still writes the missing sound."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Eight has a gh in it that nobody says. It is not decoration. English used to make a real throat scrape there, exactly the one German still makes, and the spelling is the fossil left behind after the sound fell out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So German -cht- answers English -ght-, and it does it every single time:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "German",
                "English"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "acht means eight.",
                "Nacht means night.",
                "Licht means light.",
                "recht means right."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nacht and night you already own. The pattern is worth more than the number: whenever you see a German word with cht, try the English one with ght."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"acht\" — akht, open ah then the scrape",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"acht\" — *akht*, open *ah* then the scrape]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"Nacht\" then \"acht\" — the same ending twice",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"Nacht\" then \"acht\" — the same ending twice]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the pairs — \"acht/eight\", \"Nacht/night\", \"Licht/light\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the pairs — \"acht/eight\", \"Nacht/night\", \"Licht/light\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is \"eight\"? (Acht.) What does German -cht- answer in English? (-ght-.) Why does eight have a silent gh? (English used to say the same scrape German still says.) Give another pair. (Nacht/night.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
